# Line endings, pinned so the Windows clone and the Ubuntu VM clone agree.
#
# Without this the two clones relied on each developer's core.autocrlf, which is
# per-machine config rather than a property of the repository. On Windows that
# meant every commit printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF", and the working tree
# held CRLF while the index held LF.
#
# text=auto leaves the decision about what counts as text to git's own detection,
# so it will not touch a file it identifies as binary. eol=lf then checks every
# text file out with LF on all platforms.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files where CRLF is not cosmetic but breaks execution, called out explicitly so
# the intent survives someone relaxing the blanket rule above.
#
#   Dockerfile - a trailing \r is carried into every RUN line's shell command,
#                and a backslash continuation followed by \r stops continuing.
#   *.sh       - a \r after the shebang makes the kernel search for an
#                interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return.
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.sh         text eol=lf

# No binary files are tracked in this repository today; this was checked rather
# than assumed, and every tracked file is text. The `text=auto` rule above already
# defers to git's binary detection, so an image or font committed later is not
# mangled by default. Add an explicit `*.ext binary` line here only if git's
# detection ever misclassifies a file it should have left alone.
